WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.210
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Sanford Levinson, who is a legal scholar professor

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:06.443
at the University of [inaudible] city of

00:00:06.443 --> 00:00:13.910
Texas law school and coauthor along his wife
Cynthia of the book fault lines in the Constitution,

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:19.350
the framers, their fights and the flaws that
affect us today. A Sanford. So great to talk

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.350
to you today.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.740
I'm delighted to be here.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:28.349
So to begin with, for people who may be aren't
a familiar with the genesis of the constitution,

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:34.190
what was going on in the United States that
prompted the founding fathers to realize that

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:39.379
the articles of confederation were in some
way not working or falling short, and that

00:00:39.379 --> 00:00:42.539
writing a constitution was something that
made sense.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:48.840
Right? There are three principle things. First
of all of the so called national government,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:55.750
and I use that term advisedly, wasn't capable
of getting any money. What they could do in

00:00:55.750 --> 00:01:04.420
effect was to ask the states to contribute,
but by 1786, I think they were contributing

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:13.270
something like a total of $636. So that's
the first thing, no money. Secondly, there

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:20.931
were fears of various military attacks, um,
from a variety of European countries, but

00:01:20.931 --> 00:01:27.740
also from a, a variety of Indian tribes, many
of whom for very sensible reasons had supported

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:33.080
the British during our succession from the
British empire. Um, and so there was real

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:39.860
concern about the ability of this new country
to defend itself. The third thing is that

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:45.170
there were trade wars, which is something
we're really with today in an international

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:51.500
context, but there was no established notion
of free trade among the various states. Um,

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:57.860
and there was some genuine hostility between
and among a number of the states so that people

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:03.270
like Alexander Hamilton and others described
the system of yards from the federation is

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.390
quote Imbecilic Unquote, and something had
to be done

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:15.800
when the transition from the articles of confederation
to the Constitution took place. How much discussion

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:22.510
do we know how much discussion there was about
writing the constitution in such a way to

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:27.800
indefinitely account for however society might
change? And the reason I asked that is that

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:33.800
now there is a big debate over to what degree
do we need to be amending the constitution

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:39.160
on an ongoing basis to account for how drastically
life has changed today from when it was written.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.470
Was there much discussion of accounting for
that at the time of its writing?

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:54.870
It's terrific question. I think that the most
accurate answer is no. That the first constitution

00:02:54.870 --> 00:03:02.670
that is their article federation lasted a
grand total of six years. I think that if

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:13.250
you could have interviewed the framers, they
would been thrilled to be told that the 1787

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:18.290
constitution would last 50 years. I think
if you said, well, you know, you're really

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:25.340
writing something that should last forever,
I think they would've said, you know, that's

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:34.330
crazy in part because they were extremely
aware of what they repeatedly call the lessons

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:42.370
of experience and the need to update government.
In terms of those lessons, Cynthia and I do

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:50.620
not engage and founder of ashing. We think
that the founders by and large were admirable

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:58.350
people trying to respond to what they thought
were the chief challenges of the day and that

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:05.650
they weren't good opiates. They were trying
to develop a document that could get us through

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:12.860
the next several years where several might
mean 25 or 50 but they expected amendment

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:18.530
and quite frankly, I think a lot of them expected
new conventions. Article Five, which is the

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:24.510
amendment article explicitly allows for new
conventions. And I think that most of the

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:30.280
framers would be astonished to be told that
there's been no national conventions since

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:39.410
1787 whereas there have been over 230 state
constitutional conventions since 1787

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:47.040
so there is the view from some that it is
immensely difficult to amend the constitution.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.220
So my question is sort of a two part question.
The first part is how do you assess the difficulty

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:57.690
of amending the constitution in the context
of sort of modern society? And number two,

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:04.350
uh, if indeed the founders were so welcoming
or expecting of significant amending of the

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.900
constitution as the country changed, why did
they set the level of difficulty that they

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:09.900
set?

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:16.530
Right, right. Another great question. What
they faced in something 87 was an artisan

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:23.600
convect federation that required unanimous
consent of the state legislatures of every

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:32.810
state. So from their perspective, Article
Five, which requires quote only unquote three

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:43.860
quarters of the states to set to amendment
was a big improvement. Secondly, article five

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:53.160
worked in the first, let's call it 15 or 20
years. We got the bill of rights in 1791,

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:59.810
uh, 12 amendments were sent out, 10 of them
were ratified. We call them today, the bill

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:11.260
of rights in 1800 or 1801 the problems, to
put it mildly, of the way we selected presidents

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:19.660
was revealed in the time between Thomas Jefferson
and Aaron Burr. And we got the 12th amendment,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:25.270
which changed things for the better though
not enough as far as I'm concerned. And I'm

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:31.011
skipping the 11th amendment, which is much
more esoteric. But in 1793, the supreme court

00:06:31.011 --> 00:06:37.940
issued a very controversial decision about
state's rights and it was reversed.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:43.780
So if you look at our early history, you could
say, we know three quarters is a lot, but

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:51.280
the system is more or less working in the
modern world. Three quarters of 50 where you

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:59.290
have the distribution that you now have of
small states and indeed very small states,

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:07.940
and not only large states, but very large
states and a far, far more polarized country,

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:17.210
um, than even was the case in. Um, suddenly
in 87, it has become functionally close to

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:22.280
impossible to amend the constitution. So you
know, political scientists have looked at

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:28.300
that and looked at this and some have said
that the United States national constitution

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.980
is the most difficult to amend constitution
in the world. And one of the things we talk

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:38.430
about in our book, and frankly I think one
of the things that is especially valuable

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:44.280
about our book is that we look not only at
the u s constitution, but also at the fact

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:46.780
that every state has its own constitution.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:53.380
There are 51 constitution in the United States
and if you look at the state constitutions,

00:07:53.380 --> 00:08:01.880
they are all easier to amend then the national
constitution. So it's not the case that the

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:08.910
American people are reluctant to amend their
constitution. In fact, the typical state has

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:17.600
had close to three different constitutions
over their histories. Rather, we have a peculiar

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:23.940
religious like veneration of the national
constitution that makes it almost unthinkable

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:30.230
to talk about amending it. But even if you
do think of amending, you then come up against

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:35.200
the concrete reality that it's extraordinary
difficult to get two thirds of each house

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:41.200
of Congress. And then even if you get it,
it's even more difficult to get three quarters

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:43.050
of the states.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:49.120
So one of the, um, areas that is of a, a lot
of interest right now because of the current

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:54.770
administration is the census and, uh, how
should it be carried out? What is really the

00:08:54.770 --> 00:09:01.040
underlying purpose of the census from, from
a sort of constitutional historian perspective,

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.460
what was the point of establishing the census
to begin with?

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:09.240
Since it's an extraordinarily interesting
part of the constitution, because you can

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:18.910
make an argument that it's really one of the
very, very few things Congress must make sure

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:25.021
is done. The other is actually setting up
a supreme court of the United States. But

00:09:25.021 --> 00:09:31.080
for example, setting up circuit courts or
district courts, that's optional with Congress.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.460
It's optional for Congress to declare war
or not to declare wars, et Cetera, et cetera.

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:42.570
But the constitution requires that a census
be held every 10 years. And the reason is

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:51.300
deceptively simple. It's the House of Representatives
that the house is allocated more or less proportionately

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:59.570
unlike the u s senate. Um, and so if you're
going to have a major branch of Congress where

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:06.830
representation is a function of the population
of the states, you have to know the population

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:14.540
of the states. It's really as simple as that.
And it's every 10 years because the framers

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:20.320
were smart enough to realize that the United
States was, that I NAMIC country, there would

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.550
be lots of settlement, there would be people
moving around.

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:31.350
So you couldn't necessarily depend that the
population of Virginia in 1790 relative to

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:39.350
the population of, uh, Tennessee would be
the same in 1800 or 18, 20, et Cetera, et

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:46.850
cetera. So the constitution requires every
10 years without exception. Interestingly

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:52.600
in my reading, although it requires it, it's
not clear what the penalty would be and to

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.940
whom it would be assessed if the census does
not happen as scheduled as my reading. Correct

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:04.540
of that. Well, you know, it's one of those
things, John Marshall referred to the constitution

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:11.500
as a great outline. And so there are things
that are specified. One of them is you got

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:17.899
to have a census. But again, the question
you ask is a very shrewd one. What if the

00:11:17.899 --> 00:11:26.480
president says, you know, I don't think so.
Um, then what do you do? Do you charge the

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.510
president with violating the law?

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:35.300
Well, actually this morning is being spent
on the molar hearings and hearing Robert Mower

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:41.400
saying over and over again, the Justice Department
says you can't indict a sitting president.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:53.700
So could you impeach a president for refusing
to take care to enforce one of the most basic

00:11:53.700 --> 00:12:00.130
obligations of the national government, which
is to conduct a census? I think the answer

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:10.870
is yes, but I think it would require a sense
of national outrage led by Congress that the

00:12:10.870 --> 00:12:18.750
president is being derelict with regard to
one of his most fundamental responsibilities,

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.920
which is to make sure that the house representatives
is fairly apportioned. Yes.

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:27.940
Sending in today's a hyper partisan climate.
It's hard for me to imagine that there would

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:35.370
be, uh, that level of United notice, uh, around
that particular issue. But it remains to be

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:36.370
seen. Right.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:37.399
I think you're right.

00:12:37.399 --> 00:12:41.470
We've been speaking with Sanford Levinson,
who's a legal scholar professor at the University

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:48.100
of Texas law school. The book coauthored with
his wife Cynthia, his fault lines in the constitution,

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:52.110
the framers, their fights and the flaws that
affect us today. Sanford, really a pleasure

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:53.110
having you on.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.210
Yeah, my pleasure.

